🚀 How to Use Postman in Real-Time Projects (Step-by-Step)
1. Install & Set Up
Download and install Postman
Create a free account (optional but recommended for syncing)
2. Understand Your Backend API

Before using Postman, you should already have:

Backend running (e.g., Node.js + Express)
API endpoints like:
GET /users
POST /login
PUT /profile
DELETE /user/:id
3. Create a New Request
Open Postman
Click New → HTTP Request
Choose method:
GET / POST / PUT / DELETE

Enter API URL:

http://localhost:5000/api/users
4. Add Request Data
👉 For GET
No body required
Just click Send
👉 For POST / PUT
Go to Body → raw → JSON
Example:
{
  "name": "Sagar",
  "email": "sagar@example.com"
}
5. Add Headers
Go to Headers tab
Example:
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer <token>
6. Use Environment Variables (Real Project Use 🔥)

Instead of hardcoding URLs:

Create Environment

Add variables:

BASE_URL = http://localhost:5000
TOKEN = your_jwt_token

Use like:

{{BASE_URL}}/api/users
7. Test Authentication APIs
Example: Login API
Method: POST

URL:

{{BASE_URL}}/api/auth/login
Body:
{
  "email": "test@gmail.com",
  "password": "123456"
}
Copy token from response → store in environment
8. Automate Testing (Very Important)

Go to Tests tab:

pm.test("Status code is 200", function () {
    pm.response.to.have.status(200);
});

pm.test("Response has token", function () {
    var jsonData = pm.response.json();
    pm.expect(jsonData.token).to.exist;
});
9. Create Collections (Real Project Structure)
Group APIs like:
Auth APIs
User APIs
Product APIs
10. Use Postman for Debugging

In real projects:

Check API errors quickly
Validate request/response
Test edge cases
11. Share APIs with Team
Export collection as JSON
Share via GitHub or Postman workspace
12. Use Postman in Deployment Stage

Test deployed APIs:

https://your-api.onrender.com/api/users
Validate:
Production DB
Auth
Performance